basalt VIO configs, vendored from the fork's `data/msd/`, and the RoboCap
calibration its converter wrote.

Each `[[dataset]]` in `../reference_segments.toml` names the file its segments
run with, and `slam_rs.reference.resolved_flow_config` reads it -- the C++ reference runs
were driven with these files, and basalt's constructor defaults are a different
estimator (`vio_marg_lost_landmarks`, C72).

`tests/test_cpp_reference.py` compares each file, key by key, with the
configuration every C++ `run.json` embeds, so a stale copy fails a test. The
Rust crate's config tests read these same files -- `crates/slam-rs/src/config.rs`
includes them and `tests/common/mod.rs` reads them through `configs()`.

The RoboCap pair is named by the `[robocap]` block instead of by a `[[dataset]]`:
`msdmo_config.json`, the config `python/robocap_vit.toml` runs, and
`robocap_calib_downscale3.json`, which `basalt_convert_robocap_calib.py
--downscale 3` wrote from the device's Kalibr tree. Neither is covered by the
key-by-key test above, which iterates `manifest.segments` and so never sees
them: RoboCap has no C++ `run.json` in the reference bundle. What does check
them is `tests/test_reference.py` (two substring asserts that the manifest names
files of the right kind) and, at run time,
`slam_rs.apis.robocap_probe.check_calibration_matches_recording`, which compares
the calibration against the recording's own statics camera by camera.
